---
// What a phone gives away and to whom (spec D18), read off the code and the hosted build on 2026-11-13; the sources
// are in `.scratch/outside-view/issues/14-privacy-and-imprint.md`. What the app keeps on the phone is `src/kept.ts`,
// held to the app's code by `test/privacy.test.ts`.
import brand from '../../../../brand.json';
import Detail from '../components/Detail.astro';
import { KEPT } from '../kept';
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';

/** Where the site and the app are published, and so where the browser keeps the app's data. */
const host = new URL(brand.address).host;
/**
 * The signaling relays: Trystero's default Nostr relays for the app's id (`packages/core/src/trystero-transport.ts`
 * passes no relay list; `getRelays` in `@trystero-p2p/core` picks five of its 29 by the app id).
 */
const RELAYS = ['relay.nostr.blockhenge.com', 'relay.flashapp.me', 'relay.nostrmap.net', 'relay.bullishbounty.com', 'relay.novospes.com'];
/** Trystero's default STUN servers (`@trystero-p2p/core` `peer.mjs`), kept because the app passes its Relay as `turnConfig`. */
const STUN = { google: ['stun.l.google.com', 'stun1.l.google.com', 'stun2.l.google.com'], cloudflare: 'stun.cloudflare.com' };
/** The hosted build's Relay (`VITE_MGC_TURN_SERVERS` from the repository secret, `.github/workflows/pages.yml`). */
const RELAY_HOST = 'standard.relay.metered.ca';
---

<Legal title="Privacy" description={`What ${brand.name} keeps on your phone, who your phone talks to while you play, and what each of them sees.`}>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p>
    {brand.name} has no accounts, no cookies, no analytics and no ads. It never asks who you are, and nothing you do in it
    is sent to its makers.
  </p>
  <p>
    To bring the phones of a table together over the internet, though, your phone talks to a few others. This page names
    each of them and what it sees. It was checked against the app on 13 November 2026.
  </p>

  <h2>Who your phone talks to</h2>

  <h3>GitHub, which hosts these pages</h3>
  <p>
    The site and the app are files on GitHub Pages, run by GitHub, Inc. When your phone opens them, GitHub sees its IP
    address, the addresses it opens (for a phone that joins by the QR code, that includes the table’s code) and which
    browser it uses, as the host of any website does. GitHub says it logs visitors’ IP addresses for security (<a
      href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages#data-collection"
      >GitHub Pages: data collection</a
    >, <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub’s privacy statement</a>).
  </p>

  <h3>Five public signaling relays, where phones find each other</h3>
  <p>
    Phones find their table through five public relays of the Nostr network, run by people and companies that have nothing
    to do with {brand.name}:
    {RELAYS.map((relay, i) => (<>{i > 0 && (i === RELAYS.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}<code>{relay}</code></>))}.
    They are the ones the connection library {brand.name} is built on (Trystero) picks for it; {brand.name} does not pick its own.
    While the app is open, your phone stays connected to them. A relay sees:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>your phone’s IP address;</li>
    <li>
      the table’s name, scrambled: the code itself is not sent, but a code of four letters can be worked back out by anyone
      who tries;
    </li>
    <li>
      each phone’s offer to connect, locked with a key made from the table’s code. An offer holds the network addresses a
      direct connection could use.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Anyone else connected to the same relay can see the same messages. Nothing of the game itself passes through the relays.
    Your phone signs its messages with a key it makes anew each time the app opens, so that key does not tell your visits apart.
  </p>

  <h3>Google and Cloudflare, which tell a phone its address</h3>
  <p>
    Before two phones connect, each one asks an address server (STUN) where its traffic reaches the internet from:
    {STUN.google.map((server, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ', '}<code>{server}</code></>))} (Google) and <code>{STUN.cloudflare}</code>
    (Cloudflare). They come with the same library. They see your phone’s IP address each time it sets up a connection, and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h3>Metered, which runs the Relay</h3>
  <p>
    When two phones cannot reach each other directly, as on some phone hotspots and mobile networks, their connection goes
    through a Relay: <code>{RELAY_HOST}</code>, run by Metered (Next Path Software Consulting Inc., Canada) on the makers’
    account. Each phone asks the Relay for a way through whenever it sets up a connection, so Metered sees your phone’s IP
    address each time; the game passes through it only when the direct way does not work. Then Metered also sees when the
    phones play and how much data passes. Everything that passes between the phones is encrypted, as every connection between
    browsers is, so the Relay cannot read the game. Metered says it keeps this connection data for 30 days (<a
      href="https://www.metered.ca/privacy">Metered’s privacy policy</a
    >).
  </p>

  <h3>The other phones at your table</h3>
  <p>
    The phones of a table connect to each other directly whenever they can, and like any direct connection, each one sees
    the others’ IP addresses. Your phone also sends them what the table needs: what kind of phone it is (for example “iPhone”
    or “Android”), the size of its screen, its Device id and Player id (below), the Players’ names, and your touches during a
    game. The first phone passes every phone’s messages on to the others, so it sees all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app keeps on your phone</h2>
  <p>It keeps these in your browser’s storage for {host}, not in cookies:</p>
  <ul>
    {KEPT.map((kept) => (<li><strong>{kept.what}.</strong> {kept.detail}</li>))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    Of these, the other phones of your table get what the table needs: your Device id and Player id, and from the first
    phone the table’s picks. Nothing goes to the makers or anyone else. The log leaves your phone only if someone
    exports it (in Maker mode, with Export log) and sends the file on.
  </p>

  <h2>How to delete it</h2>
  <p>
    Delete the site data for <code>{host}</code> in your browser’s settings: in Safari under Website Data, in Chrome under
    Site settings, All sites. That deletes everything above. If you added the app to your home screen, remove it from there
    too. Closing the tab deletes the table you opened. In Maker mode, Clear on the first phone’s sheet empties the log.
  </p>

  <h2>What there is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No accounts and no sign-in.</li>
    <li>No cookies: neither the site nor the app sets any, and GitHub Pages sends none.</li>
    <li>No analytics, no counting of visits, no ads.</li>
    <li>Nothing loaded from another site: the pages and their scripts, pictures and fonts all come from {host}.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    The makers of {brand.name}: <Detail of="name" />, <Detail of="contact" />. Their full details are on the <a
      href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}imprint/`}>imprint</a
    >. Ask them anything about this page. The source code is public, so every sentence here can be checked against it.
  </p>
</Legal>
